       </varlistentry>
 
+      <varlistentry>
+        <term><varname>BlockedDomainFile=</varname></term>
+
+        <listitem><para>Specifies one absolute path to a local file containing blocked DNS domain suffixes.
+        This downstream option may be repeated to load multiple files. An empty assignment resets the file list,
+        including paths specified earlier in configuration drop-ins. No files are configured by default.</para>
+
+        <para>Each file contains one domain per line. Surrounding whitespace is removed; empty lines and lines
+        beginning with <literal>#</literal> are ignored. Names are matched case-insensitively, and a trailing
+        dot is optional. A listed <literal>facebook.com</literal> blocks that name and all its subdomains,
+        including <literal>www.facebook.com</literal>, but does not block <literal>notfacebook.com</literal>
+        or <literal>facebook.com.example.org</literal>. Matching uses DNS labels. Malformed entries are
+        ignored with a warning. Wildcards, regular expressions, URLs, hosts-file entries and ad-block syntax
+        are not supported. Internationalized domains should be supplied in DNS ASCII (A-label) form.</para>
+
+        <para>Blocked questions return NXDOMAIN locally for every record type, before hosts-file and cache
+        lookup or upstream requests. This also applies to names generated by search-domain expansion and
+        CNAME/DNAME following. Responses generated by this policy are not marked DNSSEC authenticated.</para>
+
+        <para>Files are loaded at startup. Missing files are warned about and skipped at startup; other load
+        errors discard the new set and allow the resolver to start without blocking. To reread the configured
+        paths, call the privileged <literal>ReloadBlockLists()</literal> D-Bus method on
+        <literal>org.freedesktop.resolve1.Manager</literal> at <literal>/org/freedesktop/resolve1</literal>.
+        This method does not reread <filename>resolved.conf</filename> or flush DNS caches. If any file cannot
+        be opened or read, exceeds the limits, changes during reading, or contains only malformed entries,
+        the method fails and retains the entire previous set. Empty or comment-only files intentionally clear
+        their entries. Queries already in progress may finish using the previous policy.</para>
+
+        <para>Publish list updates by writing a temporary file and atomically renaming it to the configured
+        path before calling the reload method. The resolver cannot detect a syntactically valid but incomplete
+        published list. Files must be regular files readable by the <literal>systemd-resolve</literal> user.
+        At most 64 paths, 64 MiB of input per load, and 1,048,576 distinct domains are accepted. Input lines
+        must be shorter than 4096 bytes. This feature is a local resolver policy, not an enforcement mechanism
+        for applications that use other DNS servers or direct IP addresses.</para></listitem>
+      </varlistentry>
+
       <varlistentry>
         <term><varname>DNSStubListener=</varname></term>
         <listitem><para>Takes a boolean argument or one of <literal>udp</literal> and <literal>tcp</literal>. If
